CH18: Object Oriented Programming Features

Rust seems to have some design clashes with the Inheritance-based OOP.

I think it's good to start with some fundamental grounds that both parties can
agree on.
	> One major goal of inheritance is for code reuse <

The Rust book exposes one big problem of inheritance-based code-reuse: all or
nothing. When one inherits from an interface, one expects the subtype to
implement all of that interface's feature.

> Rust has chosen a different set of trade-offs by not offering inheritance.
> Inheritance is often at risk of sharing more code than necessary. Subclasses
> shouldn’t always share all characteristics of their parent class but will do
> so with inheritance. This can make a program’s design less flexible. It also
> introduces the possibility of calling methods on subclasses that don’t make
> sense or that cause errors because the methods don’t apply to the subclass.
> In addition, some languages will only allow single inheritance (meaning a
> subclass can only inherit from one class), further restricting the
> flexibility of a program’s design.

Therefore, Rust, by allowing unlimited extension, in the form of adding new
methods to a type, and by traits that can group arbitrary subset of methods,
provides a better granularity.

Indeed, in many prog langs, one can hide certain methods of an interface, by,
for example, making them throw a logic_error when invoked. Nevertheless, that
feels like an after-thought, not what originially intended by a language's
design.

